{
  "id": "dt-ztransform",
  "title": "The z-Transform",
  "description": "Turn sequences into functions of z with the geometric series, use the properties and invert with partial fractions.",
  "reference": "Phillips §11.1–11.5",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "Definition",
      "body": "The unilateral z-transform weights each sample $x[n]$, $n \\geq 0$, with $z^{-n}$, so $z^{-1}$ stands for a delay of one sample. A finite sequence becomes a polynomial in $z^{-1}$: $\\{2, 1, 3\\} \\to 2 + z^{-1} + 3z^{-2}$. For a causal sequence the sum converges outside a circle, $|z| > r$: the **region of convergence** (ROC). Results are usually rewritten in positive powers of $z$.",
      "formula": "X(z) = \\mathcal{Z}\\{x[n]\\} = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} x[n]\\,z^{-n}"
    },
    {
      "title": "Explore: from s-plane to z-plane",
      "body": "Sampling $e^{s_0t}$ every $T$ seconds gives $(e^{s_0T})^n$: a geometric sequence with a pole at $z = e^{s_0T}$. The real part $\\sigma$ sets the radius and $\\omega$ the angle. The left half s-plane maps inside the unit circle, the $j\\omega$ axis onto it and $s = 0$ to $z = 1$. Since $\\omega$ and $\\omega + 2\\pi/T$ land on the same $z$ (aliasing), $\\omega T$ only needs the range $-\\pi \\ldots \\pi$.",
      "formula": "z = e^{sT} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; |z| = e^{\\sigma T},\\quad \\angle z = \\omega T",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": -1.6,
          "max": 1.6,
          "label": "Re z"
        },
        "y": {
          "min": -1.25,
          "max": 1.25,
          "label": "Im z"
        },
        "height": 240,
        "sliders": [
          {
            "name": "sT",
            "label": "$\\sigma T$",
            "min": -1.5,
            "max": 0.4,
            "step": 0.01,
            "value": -0.3
          },
          {
            "name": "wT",
            "label": "$\\omega T$ (rad)",
            "min": -3.14,
            "max": 3.14,
            "step": 0.01,
            "value": 0.8
          }
        ],
        "curves": [
          {
            "f": "sqrt(max(0,1-x^2))",
            "from": -1,
            "to": 1,
            "color": "grey"
          },
          {
            "f": "-sqrt(max(0,1-x^2))",
            "from": -1,
            "to": 1,
            "color": "grey"
          }
        ],
        "points": [
          {
            "x": "exp(sT)*cos(wT)",
            "y": "exp(sT)*sin(wT)",
            "marker": "x",
            "color": "red",
            "guides": false,
            "label": "z"
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "label": "$|z|$",
            "value": "exp(sT)"
          },
          {
            "label": "$\\angle z$",
            "value": "wT*180/pi",
            "unit": "°"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pairs from the geometric series",
      "body": "Most pairs follow from $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} q^n = \\frac{1}{1-q}$ for $|q| < 1$. With $q = az^{-1}$ you get $a^nu[n] \\to \\frac{z}{z-a}$ for $|z| > |a|$; $a = 1$ gives the step $\\frac{z}{z-1}$. Differentiating with respect to $a$ gives the ramps, e.g. $n\\,u[n] \\to \\frac{z}{(z-1)^2}$. Also $\\delta[n] \\to 1$ and $\\delta[n-k] \\to z^{-k}$. A pole at $z = a$ means a term $a^n$ in the sequence.",
      "formula": "\\begin{aligned} a^n u[n] &\\leftrightarrow \\frac{z}{z-a} \\\\ n a^n u[n] &\\leftrightarrow \\frac{az}{(z-a)^2} \\end{aligned}"
    },
    {
      "title": "Sinusoids and damped sinusoids",
      "body": "Euler splits $\\cos\\Omega n$ into two exponentials with poles $e^{\\pm j\\Omega}$ on the unit circle. A factor $a^n$ pulls the poles in to radius $a$: $ae^{\\pm j\\Omega}$. Read the denominator $z^2 - 2az\\cos\\Omega + a^2$ backwards: $a$ is the square root of the constant term, and $\\cos\\Omega$ follows from the middle term. For $\\sin$, the numerator is $az\\sin\\Omega$.",
      "formula": "a^n\\cos\\Omega n \\to \\frac{z(z - a\\cos\\Omega)}{z^2 - 2az\\cos\\Omega + a^2}",
      "plot": {
        "x": {
          "min": -0.5,
