    {
      "title": "Shifting with initial conditions",
      "body": "A delay multiplies by $z^{-1}$, but the samples that shift in from $n < 0$ appear as extra terms. This is how initial conditions enter when you transform a difference equation. Two delays give $z^{-2}X(z) + z^{-1}x[-1] + x[-2]$. An advance pushes $x[0]$ out of the sum, so it is subtracted.",
      "formula": "\\begin{aligned} x[n-1] &\\to z^{-1}X(z) + x[-1] \\\\ x[n+1] &\\to zX(z) - zx[0] \\end{aligned}"
    },
    {
      "title": "More properties",
      "body": "The transform is linear. Multiplying by $a^n$ scales $z$; multiplying by $n$ differentiates. Convolution becomes multiplication, the reason why $Y(z) = H(z)X(z)$. Initial and final values follow straight from $X(z)$, but the final-value theorem is only valid when all poles of $(z-1)X(z)$ lie **inside** the unit circle.",
      "formula": "\\begin{aligned} a^n x[n] &\\to X(z/a) \\\\ n\\,x[n] &\\to -z\\,\\frac{dX}{dz} \\\\ x_1[n] * x_2[n] &\\to X_1(z)X_2(z) \\\\ x[0] &= \\lim_{z\\to\\infty} X(z) \\\\ x[\\infty] &= \\lim_{z\\to 1}\\,(z-1)X(z) \\end{aligned}"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inverse z-transform",
      "body": "Expand $X(z)/z$ (not $X(z)$) in partial fractions, then multiply back by $z$ so every term has the table form $\\frac{kz}{z-p} \\to kp^n$. A complex pair $p = re^{\\pm j\\Omega}$ with residues $k, k^*$ gives $2|k|\\,r^n\\cos(\\Omega n + \\angle k)$. For the first few samples, long division (in powers of $z^{-1}$) is quicker.",
      "formula": "\\begin{aligned} \\frac{X(z)}{z} &= \\sum_i \\frac{k_i}{z - p_i} \\\\ x[n] &= \\sum_i k_i\\,p_i^{\\,n} \\end{aligned}"
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "dt-ztransform-1",
      "title": "Sequences in the z-domain",
      "kind": "learn",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "i1",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "The z-transform",
          "body": "The z-transform turns a sequence into a function of the complex variable $z$. Each sample is multiplied by $z^{-n}$, so $z^{-1}$ marks a delay of one sample, just as $e^{-sT}$ does in the Laplace domain. For a finite sequence you simply write down the polynomial.",
          "formula": "\\begin{aligned} X(z) &= x[0] + x[1]z^{-1} \\\\ &\\quad {}+ x[2]z^{-2} + \\dots \\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "id": "q1",
          "type": "numeric",
          "params": {
            "x0": [
              1,
              2,
              3,
              -1
            ],
            "x1": [
              -2,
              -1,
              1,
              2,
              4
            ],
            "x2": [
              1,
              2,
              3,
              -3
            ],
            "z0": [
              2,
              -2,
              0.5,
              4
            ]
          },
          "derived": {
            "X": "x0 + x1/z0 + x2/z0^2"
          },
          "require": "abs(X) - 0.05",
          "prompt": "The sequence $x[n]$ has $x[0]$ = {{x0}}, $x[1]$ = {{x1}}, $x[2]$ = {{x2}} and is zero elsewhere. Evaluate $X(z)$ at $z$ = {{z0}}.",
          "answer": "X",
          "unit": "",
          "explanation": "$X(z) = x[0] + x[1]z^{-1} + x[2]z^{-2}$. With $z$ = {{z0}}: {{x0}} + ({{x1}})/({{z0}}) + ({{x2}})/({{z0}})² = {{x0}} + ({{x1/z0}}) + ({{x2/z0^2}}) = {{X}}."
        },
        {
          "id": "q2",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "params": {
            "N": [
              3,
              4,
              5,
              6,
              8
            ]
          },
          "prompt": "Which is the z-transform of the pulse $x[n] = u[n] - u[n-{{N}}]$ ({{N}} samples equal to 1)?",
          "options": [
            "$\\dfrac{1 - z^{-{{N}}}}{1 - z^{-1}}$",
            "$\\dfrac{z}{z - 1}$",
            "$\\dfrac{1 - z^{-{{N+1}}}}{1 - z^{-1}}$",
            "$\\dfrac{1}{1 - z^{-{{N}}}}$"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "$X(z) = \\sum_{n=0}^{{{N-1}}} z^{-n}$, a finite geometric series with ratio $z^{-1}$: $\\frac{1 - z^{-{{N}}}}{1 - z^{-1}}$. Equivalently, by linearity and the delay property: $\\frac{z}{z-1} - z^{-{{N}}}\\frac{z}{z-1}$. $\\frac{z}{z-1}$ is the endless step; the exponent ${{N+1}}$ would count one sample too many."
        },
        {
          "id": "i2",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "The geometric series",
          "body": "For $x[n] = a^n u[n]$ the sum is $\\sum (az^{-1})^n$, which converges to $\\frac{1}{1 - az^{-1}} = \\frac{z}{z-a}$ when $|az^{-1}| < 1$, i.e. for $|z| > |a|$. So a **pole at $z = a$** belongs to the sequence $a^n$: decaying if $|a| < 1$, alternating in sign if $a < 0$.",
          "formula": "a^n u[n] \\leftrightarrow \\frac{z}{z-a}, \\quad |z| > |a|"
        },
        {
          "id": "q3",
          "type": "numeric",
          "params": {
            "K": [
              2,
              3,
              5,
              10
            ],
            "a": [
              0.5,
              0.8,
              0.9,
              -0.6,
              -0.5,
              1.2
            ],
            "k": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          "prompt": "$$X(z) = \\frac{{{K}}z}{z - a}$$\nwith $a$ = {{a}}. Find $x[{{k}}]$.",
          "answer": "K*a^k",
          "unit": "",
          "explanation": "$\\frac{z}{z-a} \\leftrightarrow a^n u[n]$, so $x[n]$ = {{K}} · ({{a}})$^n$ and $x[{{k}}]$ = {{K}} × ({{a}})$^{{{k}}}$ = {{K*a^k}}."
        },
        {
          "id": "q4",
          "type": "trueFalse",
          "params": {
            "a": [
              0.5,
              -0.8,
              0.9,
              1.5,
              -2
            ],
            "zt": [
              0.6,
              -1,
              1.2,
              2.5,
              -0.4,
              3
            ]
          },
          "require": "abs(abs(zt) - abs(a)) - 0.05",
          "prompt": "The sum defining the z-transform of $x[n] = ({{a}})^n u[n]$ converges at $z$ = {{zt}}.",
          "answer": "abs(zt) - abs(a)",
          "explanation": "The series $\\sum (az^{-1})^n$ converges only for $|z| > |a|$ = {{abs(a)}}. Here $|z|$ = {{abs(zt)}}, so the statement is true exactly when $|z|$ > {{abs(a)}}, i.e. when the point lies outside the circle through the pole."
        },
        {
          "id": "i3",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "From the s-plane to the z-plane",
          "body": "Sample $e^{-\\alpha t}$ with period $T$: $x[n] = e^{-\\alpha nT} = (e^{-\\alpha T})^n$, so the s-plane pole $s = -\\alpha$ becomes the z-plane pole $z = e^{-\\alpha T}$. In general $z = e^{sT}$: stable s-plane poles ($\\sigma < 0$) land **inside the unit circle**, the $j\\omega$ axis lands on it, and the Nyquist frequency $\\omega = \\pi/T$ lands on $z = -1$.",
          "formula": "z = e^{sT}, \\qquad |z| = e^{\\sigma T}, \\quad \\angle z = \\omega T",
          "plot": {
            "x": {
              "min": -1.6,
              "max": 1.6,
              "label": "Re z"
            },
            "y": {
              "min": -1.25,
              "max": 1.25,
              "label": "Im z"
            },
            "height": 240,
            "curves": [
              {
                "f": "sqrt(max(0,1-x^2))",
                "from": -1,
                "to": 1,
                "color": "grey"
              },
              {
                "f": "-sqrt(max(0,1-x^2))",
                "from": -1,
                "to": 1,
                "color": "grey"
              }
            ],
            "points": [
              {
                "x": 1,
                "y": 0,
                "guides": false,
                "label": "s = 0"
              },
              {
                "x": -1,
